use std
into dm.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace dm). It holds the document model every reader fills: an ordered list of blocks (headings, paragraphs, list items, table rows, page markers, notes, form fields, preformatted text) with the page, sheet or slide each one sits on, plus metadata and warnings; it prints the model as Markdown or JSON, splits it into retrieval chunks and ranks the chunks for a question with BM25. Every module-level name starts with `dm_` or `DM_` because module names are used unqualified next to other modules. All text in the model is UTF-8.

```amni
import os;
```

Block kinds are module constants: `DM_HEAD = 1` (a heading, level 1 to 6), `DM_PARA = 2`, `DM_ITEM = 3` (a list item, level is the nesting depth from 1), `DM_ROW = 4` (a table row whose cells are separated by tab bytes, level is the table's number), `DM_PAGE = 5` (a page, sheet or slide marker; its text is the label), `DM_NOTE = 6` (a footnote, comment, annotation or speaker note), `DM_FIELD = 7` (a form field written "name: value"), `DM_CODE = 8` (preformatted text kept with its line breaks).

`struct DmBlock` has `kind int`, `level int`, `loc int` (the page, sheet or slide number from 1, 0 before the first marker), `at int` and `len int` (its text in `dm_pool`).

`struct DmChunk` has `at int` and `len int` (its text in `dm_ctext`), `hat int` and `hlen int` (its heading path in `dm_ctext`), `loc0 int` and `loc1 int` (first and last locator it covers) and `ntok int` (its token count).

Module state: `var dm_pool byte[>..]`, `var dm_blocks DmBlock[>..]`, `var dm_loc = 0`, `var dm_table = 0` (the number of the current table, 0 outside tables), `var dm_meta byte[>..]` (one "key", tab byte 9, "value", newline byte 10 line per entry), `var dm_warn byte[>..]` (one warning per line), `var dm_kind byte[>..]` (the format name such as "pdf"), `var dm_unit byte[>..]` (what a locator counts: "page", "sheet", "slide", "section" or "part"), `var dm_locs = 0` (the highest locator used), `var dm_ctext byte[>..]`, `var dm_chunks DmChunk[>..]`, `var dm_tmp byte[>..]` (a scratch buffer), `var dm_shift = 0` (added to the level of every `DM_HEAD` block; callers assign it directly to nest a file's headings under their own).

`dm_reset(kind const byte[:], unit const byte[:])` clears every module buffer and counter above, sets `dm_shift` to 0 and stores kind and unit.

`dm_space(c byte) bool` is true for space, tab, newline, carriage return, form feed and vertical tab.

`dm_put_cp(out byte[>..]&, cp int)` appends the Unicode code point cp to out as UTF-8 (one to four bytes); code points above 0x10FFFF, surrogates 0xD800 to 0xDFFF and 0 append the replacement character U+FFFD instead, except 0 which appends nothing.

`dm_cp1252(b int) int` maps a Windows-1252 byte to its code point: bytes below 128 and from 160 up map to themselves; 128 to 159 map through the Windows-1252 table (128 is 0x20AC, 130 0x201A, 131 0x0192, 132 0x201E, 133 0x2026, 134 0x2020, 135 0x2021, 136 0x02C6, 137 0x2030, 138 0x0160, 139 0x2039, 140 0x0152, 142 0x017D, 145 0x2018, 146 0x2019, 147 0x201C, 148 0x201D, 149 0x2022, 150 0x2013, 151 0x2014, 152 0x02DC, 153 0x2122, 154 0x0161, 155 0x203A, 156 0x0153, 158 0x017E, 159 0x0178) and the five undefined ones (129, 141, 143, 144, 157) map to themselves. Keep the 32 entries in a module `let` array of ints indexed by b minus 128.

`dm_macroman(b int) int` maps a Mac OS Roman byte to its code point: below 128 itself, 128 to 255 through the standard Mac OS Roman table kept in a module `let` array of 128 ints (0x00C4, 0x00C5, 0x00C7, 0x00C9, 0x00D1, 0x00D6, 0x00DC, 0x00E1, 0x00E0, 0x00E2, 0x00E4, 0x00E3, 0x00E5, 0x00E7, 0x00E9, 0x00E8, 0x00EA, 0x00EB, 0x00ED, 0x00EC, 0x00EE, 0x00EF, 0x00F1, 0x00F3, 0x00F2, 0x00F4, 0x00F6, 0x00F5, 0x00FA, 0x00F9, 0x00FB, 0x00FC, 0x2020, 0x00B0, 0x00A2, 0x00A3, 0x00A7, 0x2022, 0x00B6, 0x00DF, 0x00AE, 0x00A9, 0x2122, 0x00B4, 0x00A8, 0x2260, 0x00C6, 0x00D8, 0x221E, 0x00B1, 0x2264, 0x2265, 0x00A5, 0x00B5, 0x2202, 0x2211, 0x220F, 0x03C0, 0x222B, 0x00AA, 0x00BA, 0x03A9, 0x00E6, 0x00F8, 0x00BF, 0x00A1, 0x00AC, 0x221A, 0x0192, 0x2248, 0x2206, 0x00AB, 0x00BB, 0x2026, 0x00A0, 0x00C0, 0x00C3, 0x00D5, 0x0152, 0x0153, 0x2013, 0x2014, 0x201C, 0x201D, 0x2018, 0x2019, 0x00F7, 0x25CA, 0x00FF, 0x0178, 0x2044, 0x20AC, 0x2039, 0x203A, 0xFB01, 0xFB02, 0x2021, 0x00B7, 0x201A, 0x201E, 0x2030, 0x00C2, 0x00CA, 0x00C1, 0x00CB, 0x00C8, 0x00CD, 0x00CE, 0x00CF, 0x00CC, 0x00D3, 0x00D4, 0xF8FF, 0x00D2, 0x00DA, 0x00DB, 0x00D9, 0x0131, 0x02C6, 0x02DC, 0x00AF, 0x02D8, 0x02D9, 0x02DA, 0x00B8, 0x02DD, 0x02DB, 0x02C7).

`dm_utf8_ok(s const byte[:]) bool` is true when s is well-formed UTF-8 (no overlong forms, no surrogates, nothing above 0x10FFFF, no truncated sequence).

`dm_utf8_next(s const byte[:], at int&) int` decodes the code point starting at at and advances at past it; a malformed byte yields its Windows-1252 code point and advances one byte. Return -1 when at is at or past the end.

`dm_utf16(s const byte[:], big bool, out byte[>..]&)` appends the UTF-16 text s (big endian when big) to out as UTF-8, joining surrogate pairs; a lone surrogate appends U+FFFD; an odd last byte is ignored.

`dm_to_utf8(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends s as UTF-8 guessing its encoding: a UTF-8 byte order mark (239, 187, 191) is skipped and the rest copied; 255 254 means UTF-16 little endian and 254 255 big endian after the two-byte mark; with no mark, when at least a quarter of the first 512 bytes at odd positions are 0 and the length is even it is UTF-16 little endian, and at even positions UTF-16 big endian; otherwise well-formed UTF-8 (`dm_utf8_ok`) is copied as is and anything else is decoded byte by byte with `dm_cp1252`.

`dm_entity(name const byte[:]) int` returns the code point of a named HTML or XML character entity (the name without the ampersand and semicolon), or -1. It knows amp, lt, gt, quot, apos, nbsp, iexcl, cent, pound, curren, yen, brvbar, sect, uml, copy, ordf, laquo, not, shy, reg, macr, deg, plusmn, sup2, sup3, acute, micro, para, middot, cedil, sup1, ordm, raquo, frac14, frac12, frac34, iquest, times, divide, every Latin-1 letter entity from Agrave (192) to yuml (255) by its standard name, OElig, oelig, Scaron, scaron, Yuml, fnof, circ, tilde, the Greek letters Alpha to Omega and alpha to omega with thetasym, upsih and piv, ensp, emsp, thinsp, zwnj, zwj, lrm, rlm, ndash, mdash, lsquo, rsquo, sbquo, ldquo, rdquo, bdquo, dagger, Dagger, bull, hellip, permil, prime, Prime, lsaquo, rsaquo, oline, frasl, euro, image, weierp, real, trade, alefsym, larr, uarr, rarr, darr, harr, crarr, lArr, uArr, rArr, dArr, hArr, forall, part, exist, empty, nabla, isin, notin, ni, prod, sum, minus, lowast, radic, prop, infin, ang, and, or, cap, cup, int, there4, sim, cong, asymp, ne, equiv, le, ge, sub, sup, nsub, sube, supe, oplus, otimes, perp, sdot, lceil, rceil, lfloor, rfloor, lang, rang, loz, spades, clubs, hearts, diams, plus check, cross, star, hyphen, dash and the common ones larger documents use (emsp13, emsp14, numsp, puncsp, hairsp, MediumSpace, NoBreak, ZeroWidthSpace). Keep the names and code points in one ```table block or a pair of module `let` arrays and look the name up with a linear scan.

`dm_decode_entities(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends s to out replacing character references: "&#" followed by decimal digits, "&#x" or "&#X" followed by hex digits, and "&" followed by a known entity name, each ended by a semicolon (a missing semicolon is accepted for numeric references and for the names amp, lt, gt, quot and nbsp); code point 160 (no-break space) is written as a plain space; anything not recognised is copied as is.

`dm_clean(s const byte[:], keep_lines bool, out byte[>..]&)` appends a tidied copy of s: carriage returns become newlines, no-break spaces (UTF-8 194 160) and other horizontal whitespace become plain spaces, the soft hyphen (UTF-8 194 173) and zero-width characters (U+200B, U+200C, U+200D, U+FEFF) are dropped, control bytes below 32 other than tab and newline are dropped, and runs of spaces collapse to one. When keep_lines is false every newline and tab also becomes a space before collapsing, so the result is one line; when true, newlines are kept but spaces at line ends are removed and more than two newlines in a row collapse to two. Leading and trailing whitespace is trimmed.

`dm_add(kind int, level int, text const byte[:])` appends a block: for `DM_CODE` keep the text's indentation: copy it into `dm_tmp` with carriage returns turned into newlines, control bytes below 32 other than tab and newline dropped, spaces and tabs at line ends removed, and leading and trailing blank lines dropped (inner spaces are not collapsed); for every other kind clean the text into `dm_tmp` with `dm_clean` (keep_lines true for `DM_NOTE` and `DM_FIELD`, false otherwise; table rows keep their tab bytes, so for `DM_ROW` clean each tab-separated cell separately and join the cleaned cells with tab bytes, and trim empty trailing cells); when the cleaned text is empty (for a row, when every cell is empty) add nothing; otherwise append the text to `dm_pool` and push a `DmBlock` with the current `dm_loc`. A `DM_HEAD` block's level is level plus `dm_shift`, at most 6. A `DM_ROW` uses `dm_table` as its level when level is 0.

`dm_page(n int, label const byte[:])` starts locator n: set `dm_loc` to n, raise `dm_locs` to n when lower, and push a `DM_PAGE` block whose text is label (or, when label is empty, `dm_unit` with its first letter upper-cased, a space and n, like "Page 3").

`dm_new_table()` increments `dm_table`. `dm_end_table()` sets it to 0.

`dm_set_meta(key const byte[:], value const byte[:])` cleans value to one line and, when it is not empty and the key is not set yet, appends the key line to `dm_meta`. `dm_get_meta(key const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` copies the value of key into out (cleared first) and returns whether it was found.

`dm_warn_add(msg const byte[:])` appends msg and a newline to `dm_warn` unless that exact line is already there, and never more than 50 lines.

`dm_text_of(b DmBlock) const byte[:]` is the block's slice of `dm_pool`.

`dm_markdown(out byte[>..]&)` writes the model as Markdown text. First a line "# " and the title when the meta key "title" is set. Then each block in order: `DM_HEAD` as a blank line, as many "#" as its level (at most 6), a space and the text, then a blank line; `DM_PARA` as the text followed by a blank line; `DM_ITEM` as two spaces per level beyond the first, "- " and the text, then a newline (and a blank line after the last item of a run of items); `DM_ROW` as "| " followed by the cells joined with " | " and " |", a newline, and after the first row of each table (the first row whose level differs from the previous block's level or whose previous block is not a row) a separator line "|" followed by "---|" once per cell; a blank line after the last row of a table; `DM_PAGE` as a blank line, "--- ", the text, " ---" and a blank line; `DM_NOTE` as each line of the text prefixed with "> " and a blank line; `DM_FIELD` as the text and a newline (and a blank line after the last field of a run of fields); `DM_CODE` as a line "```", the text, a line "```" and a blank line. Pipes inside table cells are written as "\|". Never write more than one blank line in a row.

`dm_json_str(out byte[>..]&, s const byte[:])` appends s as a JSON string with quotes, escaping quote, backslash and control bytes (newline as \n, tab as \t, carriage return as \r, others as \u00XX).

`dm_json(out byte[>..]&)` writes the model as one JSON object: "kind" (dm_kind), "unit" (dm_unit), "count" (dm_locs), "meta" (an object of the meta keys and values in order), "warnings" (an array of the warning lines), and "blocks" (an array of objects with "t" being "h", "p", "li", "tr", "pg", "note", "field" or "code", "lv" the level, "loc" the locator and "text"; for "tr" blocks "text" is replaced by "cells", an array of the cell strings).

The chunker groups blocks into retrieval chunks. `dm_chunk(max int)` clears `dm_ctext` and `dm_chunks` and walks the blocks keeping the current heading path (the text of the latest heading at each level 1 to 6; a new heading clears the deeper levels): a heading always starts a new chunk; a page marker does not start one but updates the locator; any block that would push the current chunk's text past max bytes starts a new chunk first (a single block longer than max is split at sentence ends, ". " followed by an upper-case letter or a digit, or failing that at the last space before max, into pieces of at most max bytes each in its own chunk); table rows are written as their cells joined with " | " and each other block's text is written followed by a newline. A chunk's heading path is the non-empty heading texts joined with " > ". A chunk records the first and last locator of the blocks it holds and its token count from `dm_tokens`. Empty chunks are not kept.

`dm_lower(c byte) byte` lower-cases an ASCII letter. `dm_tok_byte(c byte) bool` is true for ASCII letters and digits and every byte from 128 up.

`dm_stem(t byte[>..]&)` applies light English stemming in place to an ASCII-lowercase token longer than 3 bytes: "ies" ending becomes "y"; "sses" becomes "ss"; a final "s" is removed unless the token ends in "ss", "us" or "is"; then "ing" is removed when at least 3 bytes remain, "ed" is removed when at least 3 bytes remain, and a final "e" is removed when at least 4 bytes remain.

`dm_stop(t const byte[:]) bool` is true for common English stop words: a, an, the, and, or, but, of, to, in, on, at, by, for, with, from, as, is, are, was, were, be, been, being, it, its, this, that, these, those, there, here, what, which, who, whom, whose, when, where, why, how, do, does, did, has, have, had, can, could, would, should, will, shall, may, might, must, i, me, my, we, our, you, your, he, she, they, them, their, his, her, him, not, no, so, if, then, than, too, very, about, into, over, under, up, down, out, all, any, some, each, also, just, only, tell, show, give, please, document, file, pdf, page, says, say, mention, mentioned.

`dm_tokens(s const byte[:], out int[>..]&)` clears out and appends one hash per token of s: a token is a maximal run of token bytes, lower-cased, stemmed with `dm_stem`, skipped when it is a stop word or a single ASCII letter; the hash is FNV-1a 32-bit of the token bytes (offset 2166136261, prime 16777619) kept in an int.

`dm_ask(q const byte[:], k int, out byte[>..]&)` ranks the chunks for the question q with Okapi BM25 (k1 = 1.2, b = 0.75) and writes the best k as JSON lines. Tokenize q with `dm_tokens` and drop duplicate hashes. For each chunk tokenize its text (once, all chunks into one flat int array with per-chunk offsets) and its heading path; a query token's term frequency in a chunk is its count in the text plus twice its count in the heading path. Document frequency is the number of chunks containing the token; idf = ln(1 + (N - df + 0.5) / (df + 0.5)) with N the chunk count; the score adds idf × tf × (k1 + 1) / (tf + k1 × (1 - b + b × len / avglen)) per query token, with len the chunk's token count and avglen the mean. Add a bonus of 0.5 × idf of the second token for every pair of consecutive query tokens that also appear consecutively in the chunk text. Keep chunks with a positive score, sort by score descending (ties by chunk order) and write at most k lines, each a JSON object with "score" (rounded to 3 decimals), "loc0", "loc1", "unit" (dm_unit), "head" and "text". When no chunk scores, write nothing.
